## 1. Wardrobe Service
 
**Responsibility:** Source of truth for all wardrobe item data. Manages full item lifecycle from purchase to retirement.
 
### Core Capabilities
- Item CRUD operations (create, read, update, delete)
- Wear event logging and tracking
- Cost-per-wear (CPW) analytics
- Lifecycle state machine management
- Wardrobe-level analytics and insights
### Key Endpoints
```
POST   /items              → Add new item (triggers Vision AI pipeline)
GET    /items              → List user items (filterable, paginated)
GET    /items/:id          → Single item detail
PATCH  /items/:id          → Update item attributes
DELETE /items/:id          → Retire/remove item
POST   /items/:id/wear     → Log wear event
GET    /items/analytics    → Wardrobe analytics (CPW, utilization, gaps)
GET    /items/lifecycle    → Items by lifecycle stage with alerts
```
 
### Lifecycle State Machine
 
Items progress through distinct lifecycle stages based on wear frequency and condition:
 
```
new_purchase
    ↓
breaking_in        (first wear logged)
    ↓
active             (≥5 wear events)
    ↓
underused          (wear rate < threshold for 60+ days)
    ↓
seasonal_hold      (manual or calendar-triggered)
    ↓
degrading          (condition_score < 0.50)
    ↓
retired            (manual action or auto-suggest)
```
 
### Business Logic
- **Wear tracking:** Each wear event updates `last_worn` timestamp and increments wear count
- **CPW calculation:** Auto-recalculated on every wear event (`purchase_price / wear_count`)
- **Condition degradation:** Auto-decremented based on material type + wear frequency model
- **Event publishing:** Kafka events published on every state transition for downstream consumers
### Data Ownership
- **Writes to:** PostgreSQL `items` table, S3 (images), Kafka `item.events` topic
- **Reads from:** PostgreSQL `items` table exclusively
